    "body": "U.S. Steel broke ground on a $1.9 billion iron production plant in Osceola, Arkansas, to supply its nearby steel mill. The project will connect the company’s Minnesota iron ore mines with steel production at its Big River Steel Works campus.\nWhat’s Related\nThe facility is expected to create about 200 full-time jobs and roughly 2,000 construction jobs at peak. Preliminary site preparation is underway, and construction is expected to start later this year.\nThe plant will produce direct reduced iron (DRI), made by removing oxygen from iron ore without melting it. It will supply Big River 2’s electric arc furnaces, which use electricity to melt scrap and iron into steel. The steel will go to customers in the automotive , appliance and solar industries.\nThe October announcement did not give a production start date. In May, company officials told the Association for Iron & Steel Technology that they expected the facility to come online in the first half of 2029.\nRELATED: U.S. Steel Invests $475M to Expand Alabama Pipe Production\nMore control over raw materials\nThe project will use iron ore pellets from U.S. Steel’s Keetac mine in Minnesota. The company will turn its own ore into DRI for its Arkansas furnaces. U.S. Steel says DRI can replace high-quality scrap used in steelmaking, helping the company manage costs and secure a more reliable supply.\n“From a rock in the ground to a coil from our mill, this facility creates a true mine-to-mill connection within U. S. Steel,” said Daniel Brown, executive vice president of advanced technology steelmaking and chief operating officer of Big River Steel Works. “It gives us greater control over a critical raw material, strengthens supply reliability and provides more flexibility to produce the advanced steels our customers need.”\nFeeding hot iron directly into the mill\nThe new plant will sit next to Big River 2, allowing iron to enter the furnaces while still hot. U.S. Steel has said that this arrangement improves efficiency and reduces electricity needs.\nThe facility will also compress the iron into solid pieces called hot briquetted iron. That form is easier to transport and can be used elsewhere on the Big River campus or shipped to other sites.\nAnother investment at Big River\nU.S. Steel announced the iron plant investment in April. Parent company Nippon Steel said the project is part of its $11 billion investment commitment.\nThe project follows the $3 billion Big River 2 expansion , which began producing steel in 2024. U.S. Steel acquired Big River Steel in 2021, and the combined campus now has four electric arc furnaces and produces more than 6 million tons of steel annually.",
